 that the  learner can deal with  and of course what a person is  interested is going to vary i may not be  interested in those stories maybe i'm  keener to fight my way through this  difficult uh political podcast but i  think a majority of people would rather  read simple stories read read read and  so there's more and more of these  readers being developed especially for  spanish which after all in the united  states is the number one  second language that's taught in the  schools  and you'll remember that there was a  fellow that came on here and said crash  and has ruined language instruction and  then he told me that he had 200 private  messages at his blog like there were no  comments  uh on this blog that he put out but he  said there were 200 private  uh messages to him saying that they  agreed with him and that the crash is  terrible and stuff like that however  bill van patten has just been elected  president of the spanish teachers  association in the united states so  perhaps it's not true that a majority of  teachers are rebelling against krashen's  ideas or bill van patten's ideas which  are largely the same but rather more  like it they are continuing to try to  get these ideas introduced in the  classroom because you can get people to  read and read a lot they're going to do  well in their own language in their own  academic or professional careers and in  learning foreign languages and we know  that in most societies 30 40 percent of  the people have poor functional literacy  skills they don't read well they  struggle with uh text that are in any  way complicated and yet in the workplace  uh even to read a manual if you're gonna  work in a factory somewhere nowadays you  need a good command of english and so  and and i mentioned too that that the  statistics of  you know the the the social cost of of  poor reading skills is tremendous people  who are in prison and so forth and so on  are overwhelmingly people who don't  read well so reading is the 